From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 10:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 82/82] local: remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_pas: restart the DSP across
 hibernation

After a cross-boot hibernate resume on x1e80100 the ADSP glink edge is dead:
"intent request timed out" every ~10 s, and battery, charging, thermal
reporting, audio and UCSI all fail with -110.

0081 is not the fix. Measured on a real cross-boot run (hibtest mode=reboot,
342 platform_pm_restore / 0 platform_pm_thaw):

  20:31:13  qcom-ipcc 408000.mailbox: re-armed 2 receive signal(s)
  20:31:18  power_supply qcom-battmgr-bat: ... `status' property: -110
  20:31:49  6800000.remoteproc:glink-edge: intent request timed out 3 times,
            treating remote as gone

The IPCC receive enable really is cleared by the reboot and 0081 really does
restore it, but the doorbell was only half the problem. The reboot also resets
the DSP, so it comes back with no glink channels open, while the restored image
still describes the edge as it was at snapshot time -- ten open channels with
their own rcids and outstanding intents. Every request after the resume is
addressed to a channel the remote has never heard of. Keep 0081; it is
necessary, it is just not sufficient.

That desync cannot be repaired from this side, which is what the four recovery
attempts in docs.local/ADSP-EL2.md were all really hitting: a fresh VERSION
draws silence, a GLINK SSR do_cleanup is never answered, and stopping the
remoteproc afterwards fails too, because the SMP2P handshake comes out of the
image so the remote never acks it and qcom_scm_pas_shutdown() then returns
-EINVAL ("failed to shutdown dtb: -22" / "can't stop rproc: -22").

So do it from the other end, while the DSP is still healthy. Measured on a
running EL1 machine, stop then start is clean and repeatable: the edge goes to
zero channels and comes back with all ten, and battmgr and UCSI recover.

  remoteproc0: stopped remote processor adsp     (no shutdown errors -- TZ
  remoteproc0: powering up adsp                   still holds a valid record)
  remoteproc0: remote processor adsp is now up

rproc_shutdown() before the snapshot unregisters the glink edge and every rpmsg
device on it, so the image has no stale channel state to be wrong about, and
rproc_boot() afterwards renegotiates the edge against the DSP that is actually
running. This works at EL1, where the DSPs are kernel-loaded via TZ/PAS
(state=running). At EL2 they are attach-only and qcom_pas_ops_no_reset has no
.start, so rproc->state is never RPROC_RUNNING there and the notifier is inert.

A PM notifier rather than dev_pm_ops on purpose: PM_HIBERNATION_PREPARE runs
before freeze_processes(), in plain process context, so unregistering the rpmsg
devices is safe. From a .freeze callback it would mutate dpm_list while the PM
core is walking it.

Known limitation, pre-existing and not introduced here: after any ADSP restart
Linux fails to ack the PDR indication for msm/adsp/audio_pd, so the DSP faults
~10 s later ("servreg_monitor.c:537:Servreg ack timedout"), takes the CDSP with
it, and both auto-recover. Audio does not come back ("can't create pcm
MultiMedia2 Playback :-110"). pdr_indack_work() ignores pdr_send_indack_msg()'s
return entirely, so the failure is silent. Battery, charging, thermal and UCSI
do come back, and audio is dead after a resume today regardless.

Set qcom_q6v5_pas.hibernate_restart=0 to disable.
---
 drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c | 112 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 112 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/mdt_loader.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/smem.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/smem_state.h>
+#include <linux/suspend.h>
 
 #include "qcom_common.h"
 #include "qcom_pil_info.h"
@@ -117,6 +118,9 @@
 
 	struct qcom_scm_pas_context *pas_ctx;
 	struct qcom_scm_pas_context *dtb_pas_ctx;
+
+	struct notifier_block pm_notifier;
+	bool hibernate_restart;
 };
 
 static void qcom_pas_segment_dump(struct rproc *rproc,
@@ -773,6 +777,106 @@
 	}
 }
 
+static bool qcom_pas_hibernate_restart = true;
+module_param_named(hibernate_restart, qcom_pas_hibernate_restart, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(hibernate_restart,
+		 "Stop the remote processor before the hibernation image is taken and boot it again on restore (default: y)");
+
+/*
+ * Hibernation: take the remote processor down before the image is written and
+ * boot it again afterwards.
+ *
+ * A cross-boot resume resets the DSP: the bootloader of the resuming kernel
+ * starts it from scratch, so it comes back with no GLINK channels open. The
+ * restored image, however, still describes the edge as it was at snapshot time
+ * -- ten open channels with their own rcids and outstanding intents. Every
+ * request after the resume is then addressed to a channel the remote has never
+ * heard of, which is why the edge looks dead in one direction while the DSP is
+ * demonstrably alive: "intent request timed out", and battery, charging,
+ * thermal reporting, audio and UCSI all fail with -110.
+ *
+ * The desync cannot be repaired after the fact. Re-arming the IPCC receive
+ * signals is necessary but not sufficient (it restores the doorbell, not the
+ * channel state), and nothing can be renegotiated from this side: a fresh
+ * VERSION draws silence and a GLINK SSR do_cleanup is never answered. Stopping
+ * the remoteproc afterwards does not work either -- the SMP2P handshake comes
+ * out of the image, so the remote never acks it, and qcom_scm_pas_shutdown()
+ * then returns -EINVAL.
+ *
+ * So do it from the other end. rproc_shutdown() while the DSP is still healthy
+ * unregisters the GLINK edge and every rpmsg device on it, so the image simply
+ * has no stale channel state to be wrong about, and the TrustZone shutdown
+ * succeeds because TZ still holds a valid record of the image we loaded. On the
+ * way back rproc_boot() authenticates the firmware again and the edge is
+ * negotiated from scratch against the DSP that is actually running.
+ */
+static int qcom_pas_pm_notify(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long action,
+			      void *data)
+{
+	struct qcom_pas *pas = container_of(nb, struct qcom_pas, pm_notifier);
+	struct rproc *rproc = pas->rproc;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!qcom_pas_hibernate_restart)
+		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
+	switch (action) {
+	case PM_HIBERNATION_PREPARE:
+	case PM_RESTORE_PREPARE:
+		/*
+		 * Runs before freeze_processes(), so this is plain process
+		 * context: unregistering the rpmsg devices here is safe, which
+		 * it would not be from a .freeze callback walking dpm_list.
+		 */
+		if (rproc->state != RPROC_RUNNING)
+			break;
+
+		ret = rproc_shutdown(rproc);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(pas->dev, "hibernate: shutdown failed: %d\n", ret);
+			break;
+		}
+
+		pas->hibernate_restart = true;
+		dev_info(pas->dev, "hibernate: stopped before the image\n");
+		break;
+
+	case PM_POST_HIBERNATION:
+	case PM_POST_RESTORE:
+		if (!pas->hibernate_restart)
+			break;
+
+		pas->hibernate_restart = false;
+
+		/*
+		 * Reached both after an ordinary hibernate and, in the restored
+		 * kernel, after a cross-boot resume -- hibernate() delivers
+		 * PM_POST_HIBERNATION once hibernation_snapshot() returns,
+		 * which is where the restored image resumes.
+		 *
+		 * In the cross-boot case the DSP now running was started by the
+		 * resuming kernel's bootloader, and TrustZone holds a record
+		 * for that image rather than for ours, so authenticating on top
+		 * of it would fail. Drop it first. This is an SCM-only
+		 * shutdown on purpose: qcom_pas_stop()'s SMP2P handshake cannot
+		 * work here. Shutting down a stopped PAS is a no-op, so it is
+		 * harmless on the paths where we really did stop it ourselves.
+		 */
+		qcom_pas_shutdown(pas->pas_id, pas->lite_pas_id);
+		if (pas->dtb_pas_id)
+			qcom_pas_shutdown(pas->dtb_pas_id, pas->lite_dtb_pas_id);
+
+		ret = rproc_boot(rproc);
+		if (ret)
+			dev_err(pas->dev, "hibernate: boot after restore failed: %d\n", ret);
+		else
+			dev_info(pas->dev, "hibernate: booted after restore\n");
+		break;
+	}
+
+	return NOTIFY_DONE;
+}
+
 static int qcom_pas_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	const struct qcom_pas_data *desc;
@@ -934,8 +1038,15 @@
 	if (ret)
 		goto remove_ssr_sysmon;
 
+	pas->pm_notifier.notifier_call = qcom_pas_pm_notify;
+	ret = register_pm_notifier(&pas->pm_notifier);
+	if (ret)
+		goto del_rproc;
+
 	return 0;
 
+del_rproc:
+	rproc_del(rproc);
 remove_ssr_sysmon:
 	qcom_remove_ssr_subdev(rproc, &pas->ssr_subdev);
 	qcom_remove_sysmon_subdev(pas->sysmon);
@@ -963,6 +1074,7 @@
 	if (!pas->rproc)
 		return;
 
+	unregister_pm_notifier(&pas->pm_notifier);
 	rproc_del(pas->rproc);
 
 	qcom_q6v5_deinit(&pas->q6v5);
-- 
